      "body": "`AsyncMPClient._engine_status` has two writers and they were stepping on each other.\n\nThe engine core republishes its whole status payload on every push. The client's own\n`handle_fault()` also writes `last_ft_request_id` and `ft_error` into the same entry when an FT\napply fails (`core_client.py:1444-1445`). The push did `_engine_status[idx] = payload`, so the\nnext status update from the engine core wiped both client keys, and the engine core never\nresends them because it does not know they exist.\n\nThe user-visible effect is that `GET /fault_tolerance/status` loses the only record of why a\nrecovery attempt failed, which is exactly when an operator wants it.\n\nIt also makes an existing test racy. `tests/v1/fault_tolerance/test_fault_tolerance_e2e.py`\nwaits for `last_ft_request_id` to appear and then asserts `\"status is DEAD\" in ft_error`. That\nonly passes if no engine status push lands in between.\n\n## The fix\n\nA small helper that replaces the engine-owned keys and carries the two client-owned ones over.\n\nI deliberately did not use `entry.update(payload)`. A plain merge would leave a stale\n`fault_info` behind after a recovery push, which trades one wrong answer for another. Replacing\nand carrying over keeps the engine core authoritative for everything it actually sends, and a\npush that carries `ft_error` itself still wins.\n\n## Test\n\nNew `tests/v1/engine/test_engine_status_cache.py`, five cases: the carry-over, the replace of\nengine-owned keys, a push for an engine not yet in the cache, a push that carries a client key\nitself, and that other engines' entries are untouched. It lands in a directory\n`test_areas/engine.yaml` already globs, so no CI config change.\n\nRan the five tests against the function as it appears in the patched file, plus a negative\ncontrol with the old body, which fails on `KeyError: 'last_ft_request_id'`. ruff, format, SPDX\nand test tethering all pass.\n",
